1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,377
Every morning, you and a friend get coffee together.

2
00:00:03,377 --> 00:00:06,320
And every morning, you flip a coin to decide who pays.

3
00:00:06,670 --> 00:00:08,335
It's always your friend's coin.

4
00:00:08,335 --> 00:00:10,240
And your friend always calls heads.

5
00:00:10,590 --> 00:00:12,661
After a month, you look back.

6
00:00:12,661 --> 00:00:15,430
Twenty flips. Heads came up fifteen times.

7
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:17,929
So you bought fifteen of the coffees.

8
00:00:17,929 --> 00:00:19,220
Your friend bought just five.

9
00:00:20,220 --> 00:00:22,181
The first few times, it seemed like luck.

10
00:00:22,181 --> 00:00:23,534
Coins are random, right?

11
00:00:23,534 --> 00:00:25,800
But by the end of the month, you start to wonder.

12
00:00:26,150 --> 00:00:27,475
So here's the question.

13
00:00:27,475 --> 00:00:30,310
Is that coin rigged? Or did your friend just get lucky?

14
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:32,860
Take a second, and decide what you think.

15
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:37,079
Your gut probably says something is off.

16
00:00:37,079 --> 00:00:40,024
But a fair coin can come up heads fifteen times.

17
00:00:40,024 --> 00:00:41,380
It's not impossible.

18
00:00:41,730 --> 00:00:44,830
So how unusual is fifteen out of twenty, really?

19
00:00:44,830 --> 00:00:46,950
Unusual enough to call it rigged?

20
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:49,872
There is a tool built for exactly this question.

21
00:00:49,872 --> 00:00:53,620
It's called hypothesis testing, and the number it gives you is

22
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:54,820
called a p value.

23
00:00:55,820 --> 00:00:57,204
Welcome to Math Lab TV.

24
00:00:57,204 --> 00:00:59,170
Where math is something you can see.

25
00:00:59,870 --> 00:01:02,125
Today you will learn how to test a claim,

26
00:01:02,125 --> 00:01:04,990
by asking what boring old chance would usually do.

27
00:01:05,340 --> 00:01:07,745
You will see exactly what a p value measures,

28
00:01:07,745 --> 00:01:09,310
as an area you can point at.

29
00:01:09,660 --> 00:01:12,640
And you will see the two ways a test can be wrong,

30
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:14,930
and the traps that fool even scientists.

31
00:01:15,530 --> 00:01:18,140
In our Bayes video, we left you with a puzzle.

32
00:01:18,490 --> 00:01:21,252
If you missed it, you'll find it in the card at the top of this

33
00:01:21,252 --> 00:01:21,790
video.

34
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:25,533
Scientists often report a p value, like zero point zero three,

35
00:01:25,533 --> 00:01:28,486
and it gets described as a three percent chance that the result

36
00:01:28,486 --> 00:01:29,260
is a fluke.

37
00:01:29,610 --> 00:01:31,964
We said that description flips the question.

38
00:01:31,964 --> 00:01:35,179
Today, we'll see what a p value really measures,

39
00:01:35,179 --> 00:01:37,320
and you'll see the flip with your own eyes.

40
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:41,410
And this matters, because p values are everywhere.

41
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:44,768
A new medicine is tested against a sugar pill.

42
00:01:44,768 --> 00:01:46,979
A website tries a new button colour.

43
00:01:46,979 --> 00:01:49,390
A trader checks if a strategy beats luck.

44
00:01:49,740 --> 00:01:52,757
Every one of them asks the same thing as you and your friend.

45
00:01:52,757 --> 00:01:54,770
Is this a real effect, or just chance?

46
00:01:55,770 --> 00:01:58,342
Hypothesis testing starts in a strange place.

47
00:01:58,342 --> 00:02:01,982
Not with what you suspect, but with the most boring explanation

48
00:02:01,982 --> 00:02:02,630
there is.

49
00:02:02,980 --> 00:02:05,891
For our coin, the boring explanation is simple.

50
00:02:05,891 --> 00:02:09,419
The coin is fair. Heads and tails are equally likely,

51
00:02:09,419 --> 00:02:11,080
and fifteen heads was just luck.

52
00:02:11,430 --> 00:02:14,108
This boring explanation has a name.

53
00:02:14,108 --> 00:02:16,540
It's called the null hypothesis.

54
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:17,989
Null just means nothing.

55
00:02:17,989 --> 00:02:19,930
Nothing special is going on.

56
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:22,593
Your suspicion, that the coin favours heads,

57
00:02:22,593 --> 00:02:25,060
is called the alternative hypothesis.

58
00:02:25,410 --> 00:02:28,375
Here's the key idea. We don't try to prove the alternative

59
00:02:28,375 --> 00:02:31,952
directly. Instead, we pretend the boring explanation is true,

60
00:02:31,952 --> 00:02:34,060
and ask how surprising our result would be.

61
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:36,293
It's just like a courtroom.

62
00:02:36,293 --> 00:02:38,370
The defendant is presumed innocent.

63
00:02:38,370 --> 00:02:41,409
The prosecution has to show the evidence would be very hard to

64
00:02:41,409 --> 00:02:42,980
explain if they were innocent.

65
00:02:43,330 --> 00:02:46,677
The null hypothesis is the innocent defendant.

66
00:02:46,677 --> 00:02:49,130
And our fifteen heads is the evidence.

67
00:02:50,130 --> 00:02:53,788
So, if the coin really is fair, what would twenty flips usually

68
00:02:53,788 --> 00:02:54,410
look like?

69
00:02:54,760 --> 00:02:57,010
Let's find out with a thought experiment.

70
00:02:57,010 --> 00:03:00,496
Imagine a thousand friends, each with a perfectly fair coin,

71
00:03:00,496 --> 00:03:02,340
each flipping it twenty times.

72
00:03:02,690 --> 00:03:03,931
Each friend counts their heads.

73
00:03:03,931 --> 00:03:06,600
Then we stack them up by how many heads they got.

74
00:03:06,950 --> 00:03:08,506
This picture has a name.

75
00:03:08,506 --> 00:03:10,508
It's called a distribution.

76
00:03:10,508 --> 00:03:14,515
It just shows every possible result, and how often each one

77
00:03:14,515 --> 00:03:15,180
happens.

78
00:03:15,530 --> 00:03:18,320
Look at the shape. Most friends land near ten heads,

79
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:22,300
the middle. About one hundred and seventy six of them get

80
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:23,460
exactly ten.

81
00:03:23,810 --> 00:03:26,887
Results further from ten get rarer and rarer,

82
00:03:26,887 --> 00:03:28,060
on both sides.

83
00:03:28,410 --> 00:03:31,870
In our standard deviation video, we learned to measure spread.

84
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:34,240
That one is in the card at the top too.

85
00:03:34,590 --> 00:03:38,320
Here, the typical spread is about two heads either side of ten.

86
00:03:38,670 --> 00:03:40,250
So, where does your friend's fifteen land?

87
00:03:41,050 --> 00:03:43,769
Way out here. More than two spreads from the middle.

88
00:03:43,769 --> 00:03:47,416
Only about fifteen of the thousand fair coins got exactly

89
00:03:47,416 --> 00:03:48,320
fifteen heads.

90
00:03:48,670 --> 00:03:51,776
But we don't just ask about exactly fifteen.

91
00:03:51,776 --> 00:03:55,102
We ask: how many fair coins did at least this well?

92
00:03:55,102 --> 00:03:59,830
Fifteen heads, or sixteen, or seventeen, all the way to twenty.

93
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:01,380
Let's fill in that tail.

94
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:04,560
About twenty one friends out of a thousand.

95
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:06,220
That's roughly two in a hundred.

96
00:04:07,220 --> 00:04:08,679
And that is the p value.

97
00:04:08,679 --> 00:04:10,550
Zero point zero two one.

98
00:04:12,050 --> 00:04:15,575
In plain words: if the coin were fair, a result this extreme or

99
00:04:15,575 --> 00:04:18,880
more would happen only about two times in a hundred.

100
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:22,138
We don't actually need a thousand friends.

101
00:04:22,138 --> 00:04:25,604
There are about a million different ways twenty flips can come

102
00:04:25,604 --> 00:04:28,490
out, all equally likely for a fair coin.

103
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:32,160
Exactly twenty one thousand seven hundred of them have fifteen

104
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:35,551
heads or more. Divide, and you get the same zero point zero two

105
00:04:35,551 --> 00:04:36,140
one.

106
00:04:36,490 --> 00:04:40,076
Let's give it symbols. People write p equals the probability of

107
00:04:40,076 --> 00:04:43,662
data at least this extreme, given that the null hypothesis is

108
00:04:43,662 --> 00:04:44,080
true.

109
00:04:44,430 --> 00:04:47,489
The capital P means probability.

110
00:04:47,489 --> 00:04:50,291
H zero is the null hypothesis.

111
00:04:50,291 --> 00:04:53,570
And the straight line means given, or assuming.

112
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:55,169
Remember that straight line.

113
00:04:55,169 --> 00:04:56,590
It's going to matter a lot.

114
00:04:57,590 --> 00:05:01,086
One honest detail. We only counted the heads side,

115
00:05:01,086 --> 00:05:03,489
because your suspicion was about heads.

116
00:05:03,489 --> 00:05:06,983
If you'd suspected the coin was off in either direction,

117
00:05:06,983 --> 00:05:10,040
you'd count both tails, and the p value would double to about

118
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:11,570
zero point zero four.

119
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:15,104
So, two in a hundred. Is that rare enough to call the coin

120
00:05:15,104 --> 00:05:15,690
rigged?

121
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:19,286
To decide, people choose a cut off before they look at the data.

122
00:05:19,286 --> 00:05:22,313
It's called the significance level, and it's written with the

123
00:05:22,313 --> 00:05:23,410
Greek letter alpha.

124
00:05:23,760 --> 00:05:26,394
The most common choice is zero point zero five.

125
00:05:26,394 --> 00:05:27,620
One in twenty.

126
00:05:27,970 --> 00:05:30,927
The rule is: if the p value is smaller than alpha,

127
00:05:30,927 --> 00:05:34,051
we say the result is statistically significant,

128
00:05:34,051 --> 00:05:36,400
and we reject the boring explanation.

129
00:05:36,750 --> 00:05:39,963
Our p value is zero point zero two one.

130
00:05:39,963 --> 00:05:42,428
That's below zero point zero five.

131
00:05:42,428 --> 00:05:46,610
So at this line, we'd reject the idea that the coin is fair.

132
00:05:47,610 --> 00:05:50,089
But here's something most people never hear.

133
00:05:50,089 --> 00:05:52,158
That line is not a law of nature.

134
00:05:52,158 --> 00:05:53,610
Somebody chose it.

135
00:05:53,960 --> 00:05:58,029
The statistician Ronald Fisher suggested one in twenty about a

136
00:05:58,029 --> 00:06:01,113
hundred years ago, as a convenient rule of thumb.

137
00:06:01,113 --> 00:06:01,740
It stuck.

138
00:06:02,090 --> 00:06:04,618
Other fields draw the line in very different places.

139
00:06:04,618 --> 00:06:07,421
Particle physicists want something like one in three and a half

140
00:06:07,421 --> 00:06:09,620
million before they announce a discovery.

141
00:06:09,970 --> 00:06:12,419
And watch what the line does to our coin.

142
00:06:12,419 --> 00:06:16,849
Fifteen heads gives a p value of zero point zero two one.

143
00:06:16,849 --> 00:06:18,060
Significant.

144
00:06:18,410 --> 00:06:21,916
Fourteen heads gives about zero point zero five eight.

145
00:06:21,916 --> 00:06:23,310
Not significant.

146
00:06:23,660 --> 00:06:27,286
One coin flip apart. The evidence barely changed,

147
00:06:27,286 --> 00:06:29,630
but the verdict flipped completely.

148
00:06:31,130 --> 00:06:34,533
So treat significant and not significant as a rough sign,

149
00:06:34,533 --> 00:06:37,758
not a magic switch. Always look at the p value itself,

150
00:06:37,758 --> 00:06:39,570
and at the size of the effect.

151
00:06:40,570 --> 00:06:44,287
Now that we know what a p value is, let's be very clear about

152
00:06:44,287 --> 00:06:45,600
three things it is not.

153
00:06:45,950 --> 00:06:49,580
Number one. It is not the chance that the coin is fair.

154
00:06:49,930 --> 00:06:54,429
A p value of zero point zero two one does not mean a two percent

155
00:06:54,429 --> 00:06:58,420
chance the coin is fair, or a two percent chance it was a fluke.

156
00:06:58,770 --> 00:07:00,436
Remember the straight line.

157
00:07:00,436 --> 00:07:04,972
The p value assumes the coin is fair, then asks about the data.

158
00:07:04,972 --> 00:07:07,120
It does not ask about the coin.

159
00:07:07,470 --> 00:07:09,600
This is the same flip we saw with Bayes.

160
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:12,783
The chance a sick person tests positive is not the chance a

161
00:07:12,783 --> 00:07:14,488
positive person is sick.

162
00:07:14,488 --> 00:07:17,671
And the chance of this data from a fair coin is not the chance

163
00:07:17,671 --> 00:07:18,740
the coin is fair.

164
00:07:20,240 --> 00:07:23,480
Number two. It is not a measure of how big the effect is.

165
00:07:23,830 --> 00:07:27,050
A tiny p value does not mean the coin is very rigged.

166
00:07:27,050 --> 00:07:28,670
We'll see why in a moment.

167
00:07:29,020 --> 00:07:32,719
Number three. A big p value does not prove the boring

168
00:07:32,719 --> 00:07:34,010
explanation.

169
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:38,796
Suppose you flip a coin only five times, and get four heads.

170
00:07:38,796 --> 00:07:42,433
The p value is large. That doesn't show the coin is fair.

171
00:07:42,433 --> 00:07:45,050
It just shows five flips can't tell you much.

172
00:07:46,050 --> 00:07:49,239
In a courtroom, not guilty doesn't mean innocent.

173
00:07:49,239 --> 00:07:51,520
It means there wasn't enough evidence.

174
00:07:52,520 --> 00:07:56,120
Any test that makes a yes or no decision can be wrong in two

175
00:07:56,120 --> 00:07:57,140
different ways.

176
00:07:57,490 --> 00:08:01,510
The first way: the coin is fair, but we call it rigged anyway.

177
00:08:01,510 --> 00:08:05,520
That's a false alarm. Statisticians call it a type one error.

178
00:08:05,870 --> 00:08:08,720
In the courtroom, that's convicting an innocent person.

179
00:08:09,070 --> 00:08:11,844
The second way: the coin really is rigged,

180
00:08:11,844 --> 00:08:13,982
but we say it's fair. That's a miss.

181
00:08:13,982 --> 00:08:15,900
It's called a type two error.

182
00:08:16,250 --> 00:08:19,170
In the courtroom, that's letting a guilty person walk free.

183
00:08:20,170 --> 00:08:22,098
Here's what alpha really controls.

184
00:08:22,098 --> 00:08:25,005
With our rule, call it rigged at fifteen heads or more,

185
00:08:25,005 --> 00:08:28,610
a fair coin gets falsely accused only about two times in a

186
00:08:28,610 --> 00:08:29,210
hundred.

187
00:08:29,560 --> 00:08:32,327
Notice that it's two in a hundred, not five.

188
00:08:32,327 --> 00:08:35,086
Twenty flips can only give whole numbers of heads,

189
00:08:35,086 --> 00:08:38,268
so the line can't sit exactly at one in twenty.

190
00:08:38,268 --> 00:08:40,180
It lands on the nearest safe step.

191
00:08:40,530 --> 00:08:44,166
But what about misses? Suppose the coin really is rigged,

192
00:08:44,166 --> 00:08:46,730
and lands heads seventy percent of the time.

193
00:08:47,080 --> 00:08:50,573
Then fifteen heads or more happens only about forty two percent

194
00:08:50,573 --> 00:08:54,110
of the time. So more than half the time, our test would miss it.

195
00:08:55,610 --> 00:08:57,730
Twenty flips just isn't much evidence.

196
00:08:57,730 --> 00:08:59,850
Both pictures overlap a lot.

197
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:02,330
Now flip a hundred times instead.

198
00:09:02,330 --> 00:09:04,247
The two pictures pull apart.

199
00:09:04,247 --> 00:09:08,074
With a cut off at fifty nine heads, a fair coin is still falsely

200
00:09:08,074 --> 00:09:10,840
accused under five times in a hundred.

201
00:09:11,190 --> 00:09:14,322
And the rigged coin gets caught about ninety nine times in a

202
00:09:14,322 --> 00:09:14,910
hundred.

203
00:09:16,410 --> 00:09:20,171
That's the trade off. A stricter line means fewer false alarms

204
00:09:20,171 --> 00:09:24,184
but more misses. And the main way to cut both at once is more

205
00:09:24,184 --> 00:09:24,910
data.

206
00:09:25,910 --> 00:09:29,070
Now let's come back to that second thing a p value is not.

207
00:09:29,070 --> 00:09:30,340
A measure of size.

208
00:09:30,690 --> 00:09:32,630
Imagine a different coin.

209
00:09:32,630 --> 00:09:36,075
It's almost fair. It lands heads fifty point two percent of the

210
00:09:36,075 --> 00:09:38,020
time. Just a hair more than half.

211
00:09:38,370 --> 00:09:40,746
Over twenty flips, you'd never notice.

212
00:09:40,746 --> 00:09:43,484
But suppose a machine flips it a million times,

213
00:09:43,484 --> 00:09:45,640
and gets five hundred and two thousand heads.

214
00:09:45,990 --> 00:09:50,251
For a fair coin, the spread over a million flips is about five

215
00:09:50,251 --> 00:09:54,007
hundred heads. Two thousand extra heads is four spreads from the

216
00:09:54,007 --> 00:09:54,480
middle.

217
00:09:54,830 --> 00:09:58,366
The p value is about zero point zero zero zero zero three.

218
00:09:58,366 --> 00:09:59,876
Three in a hundred thousand.

219
00:09:59,876 --> 00:10:01,570
Incredibly significant.

220
00:10:02,570 --> 00:10:05,368
But does it matter? If this coin decided your coffee,

221
00:10:05,368 --> 00:10:08,490
you'd pay for about two extra coffees in every thousand.

222
00:10:09,990 --> 00:10:11,719
And it works the other way too.

223
00:10:11,719 --> 00:10:14,201
A big effect can hide behind a big p value,

224
00:10:14,201 --> 00:10:16,119
when there's only a little data.

225
00:10:16,119 --> 00:10:19,167
That was our seventy percent coin, missed more than half the

226
00:10:19,167 --> 00:10:20,330
time in twenty flips.

227
00:10:21,330 --> 00:10:24,776
So the p value tells you whether an effect is probably there.

228
00:10:24,776 --> 00:10:28,020
It does not tell you whether it's big enough to care about.

229
00:10:28,370 --> 00:10:32,030
With enough data, almost any tiny difference becomes

230
00:10:32,030 --> 00:10:35,286
significant. That's why good reports show both the p value and

231
00:10:35,286 --> 00:10:36,720
the size of the effect.

232
00:10:37,720 --> 00:10:39,340
So where will you actually meet this?

233
00:10:39,690 --> 00:10:42,627
In data science, the classic one is an A B test.

234
00:10:42,627 --> 00:10:45,920
Half the visitors see the old button, half see the new one.

235
00:10:46,270 --> 00:10:50,111
The null hypothesis is that the button makes no difference.

236
00:10:50,111 --> 00:10:53,747
If the new one gets more clicks, the p value asks: how often

237
00:10:53,747 --> 00:10:56,310
would a gap this big appear by chance alone?

238
00:10:56,660 --> 00:11:00,159
In machine learning, you compare two models on the same test

239
00:11:00,159 --> 00:11:02,567
set. One scores a little higher.

240
00:11:02,567 --> 00:11:06,284
Is it really better, or did it just get a luckier set of

241
00:11:06,284 --> 00:11:07,160
examples?

242
00:11:07,510 --> 00:11:10,236
A hypothesis test, or a simple shuffle test,

243
00:11:10,236 --> 00:11:13,780
tells you whether that gap is bigger than chance usually makes.

244
00:11:14,130 --> 00:11:18,951
And when you fit a linear regression, every input gets its own p

245
00:11:18,951 --> 00:11:22,759
value. It asks: if this input truly had no effect,

246
00:11:22,759 --> 00:11:25,400
how surprising would the slope we found be?

247
00:11:25,750 --> 00:11:29,449
In finance, a trader backtests a strategy and it beats the

248
00:11:29,449 --> 00:11:31,846
market. But markets are noisy.

249
00:11:31,846 --> 00:11:35,545
The null hypothesis is that the strategy has no real edge,

250
00:11:35,545 --> 00:11:37,070
and the profit was luck.

251
00:11:37,420 --> 00:11:41,114
And try enough strategies, and some will look brilliant by pure

252
00:11:41,114 --> 00:11:43,870
luck. We'll come back to that in a moment.

253
00:11:44,220 --> 00:11:47,751
In computer science, engineers test whether new code is really

254
00:11:47,751 --> 00:11:51,308
faster. Timing a program is noisy, just like a coin.

255
00:11:51,308 --> 00:11:55,046
So they run it many times, and test whether the speed up is more

256
00:11:55,046 --> 00:11:55,680
than noise.

257
00:11:56,680 --> 00:11:59,939
Different worlds, same question as your coffee.

258
00:11:59,939 --> 00:12:02,070
Real effect, or just chance?

259
00:12:03,070 --> 00:12:05,325
Let's finish with the most common mistakes,

260
00:12:05,325 --> 00:12:07,950
because they trip up beginners and experts alike.

261
00:12:08,300 --> 00:12:12,006
Mistake one: reading the p value as the chance the boring

262
00:12:12,006 --> 00:12:13,620
explanation is true.

263
00:12:13,970 --> 00:12:16,444
Here's how far apart those two numbers can be.

264
00:12:16,444 --> 00:12:20,265
Suppose that in your town, only one coin in a hundred is rigged,

265
00:12:20,265 --> 00:12:23,490
and a rigged one lands heads seventy percent of the time.

266
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:26,273
Then, just like in our Bayes theorem video,

267
00:12:26,273 --> 00:12:28,906
which is linked in the card above, we count.

268
00:12:28,906 --> 00:12:31,717
Among all the coins that would give fifteen heads or more,

269
00:12:31,717 --> 00:12:34,329
about eighty three percent are still fair coins.

270
00:12:34,329 --> 00:12:37,140
Only about seventeen percent are rigged.

271
00:12:37,490 --> 00:12:39,028
The p value was two percent.

272
00:12:39,028 --> 00:12:42,449
But the chance the coin is rigged is only about one in six.

273
00:12:42,449 --> 00:12:44,960
Same evidence, very different numbers.

274
00:12:46,460 --> 00:12:50,377
Mistake two: running lots of tests, and only reporting the one

275
00:12:50,377 --> 00:12:51,210
that worked.

276
00:12:51,560 --> 00:12:54,112
If you test twenty things that have no real effect,

277
00:12:54,112 --> 00:12:57,248
each at one in twenty, the chance that at least one looks

278
00:12:57,248 --> 00:12:59,800
significant is about sixty four percent.

279
00:13:00,150 --> 00:13:04,098
This has a name. It's called p hacking: trying analysis after

280
00:13:04,098 --> 00:13:07,000
analysis until something crosses the line.

281
00:13:07,350 --> 00:13:11,115
That's how a lucky trading strategy, or a surprising headline,

282
00:13:11,115 --> 00:13:12,313
can come from pure noise.

283
00:13:12,313 --> 00:13:15,100
If you test many things, you need a stricter line.

284
00:13:16,100 --> 00:13:19,267
Mistake three: treating not significant as proof there's no

285
00:13:19,267 --> 00:13:19,880
effect.

286
00:13:20,230 --> 00:13:22,355
Remember the seventy percent coin.

287
00:13:22,355 --> 00:13:25,272
With twenty flips, we missed it more than half the time.

288
00:13:25,272 --> 00:13:28,160
Not significant often just means not enough data.

289
00:13:29,160 --> 00:13:32,435
And a bonus mistake: choosing the line after you've seen the

290
00:13:32,435 --> 00:13:36,337
data. If you move alpha until your result is significant,

291
00:13:36,337 --> 00:13:38,390
the p value stops meaning anything.

292
00:13:39,390 --> 00:13:42,820
Let's turn all of that into a recipe you can use for any claim.

293
00:13:43,170 --> 00:13:45,960
Step one: state the boring explanation.

294
00:13:45,960 --> 00:13:47,675
That's the null hypothesis.

295
00:13:47,675 --> 00:13:49,390
Nothing special is going on.

296
00:13:49,740 --> 00:13:52,318
Step two: choose your line, before you look.

297
00:13:52,318 --> 00:13:54,890
Alpha, usually one in twenty.

298
00:13:55,240 --> 00:13:58,371
Step three: picture what chance alone would do.

299
00:13:58,371 --> 00:14:02,310
Draw the distribution, assuming the null is true.

300
00:14:02,660 --> 00:14:04,275
Step four: find the tail.

301
00:14:04,275 --> 00:14:08,035
The p value is the share of that picture at least as extreme as

302
00:14:08,035 --> 00:14:08,960
your result.

303
00:14:09,310 --> 00:14:12,183
Step five: decide, and then ask how big.

304
00:14:12,183 --> 00:14:16,584
Compare p to alpha, and always report the size of the effect

305
00:14:16,584 --> 00:14:17,050
too.

306
00:14:18,550 --> 00:14:21,101
For the coffee coin: fair coin as the null.

307
00:14:21,101 --> 00:14:22,963
Fifteen heads out of twenty.

308
00:14:22,963 --> 00:14:25,764
A p value of about two in a hundred.

309
00:14:25,764 --> 00:14:29,488
Below one in twenty, so we'd call it suspicious,

310
00:14:29,488 --> 00:14:31,350
and keep flipping to be sure.

311
00:14:32,350 --> 00:14:34,597
A p value doesn't tell you what's true.

312
00:14:34,597 --> 00:14:37,350
It tells you how surprised chance would be.

313
00:14:39,350 --> 00:14:42,860
Look around your world, and notice how often a lucky streak gets

314
00:14:42,860 --> 00:14:44,520
mistaken for a pattern.

315
00:14:45,120 --> 00:14:47,234
If this lesson made things clear for you,

316
00:14:47,234 --> 00:14:50,189
please subscribe to Math Lab TV, so we can keep building

317
00:14:50,189 --> 00:14:51,040
together.

318
00:14:51,840 --> 00:14:53,500
But here's a puzzle for next time.

319
00:14:53,850 --> 00:14:57,467
In a courtroom, an expert says there's only a one in a million

320
00:14:57,467 --> 00:15:00,190
chance that an innocent person would match the evidence.

321
00:15:00,540 --> 00:15:03,473
So the jury thinks there's a one in a million chance the

322
00:15:03,473 --> 00:15:07,030
defendant is innocent. Real cases have gone exactly this way.

323
00:15:07,380 --> 00:15:09,612
It's the very same flip we saw today.

324
00:15:09,612 --> 00:15:13,272
Next time, we'll see why it's called the prosecutor's fallacy,

325
00:15:13,272 --> 00:15:14,900
and why smart people fall for it.

326
00:15:15,250 --> 00:15:16,750
I will see you in the next video.
